  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Wall, dummy or third man",
  "description": "Five against five on about 40 × 30 metres. At one end is a full-size goal, and the team defending it has one of its five in goal. At the other end are two cone goals about five metres wide, which the other team defends without a goalkeeper; goals there are scored by dribbling through. Swap at half-time so both teams attack the big goal.\n\nThe team on the ball looks for one-twos. A goal is worth one point, a goal after a one-two two points, and a goal where the wall player instead played on to a third player three points. The player on the ball can also show the wall pass, dummy it and go alone.\n\nVariation: limit to two touches for a while, or try a delayed one-two where the wall player takes a touch and waits for the run before playing it back. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Play the wall pass to the foot furthest from the defender."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Start the run as the ball leaves your foot – past the defender on their blind side."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Wall player: look before the ball arrives. If the defender follows the runner, turn or find a third player."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Defender sitting on the wall player? Dummy the pass and dribble yourself."
    }
  ],
